const DECKS=[
{id:"basics", title:"Samples and statistics", sub:"Population, parameter, statistic, sampling distribution", cards:[
 {f:"Population and parameter", b:"The <b>population</b> is the entire set of units we want to learn about. A <b>parameter</b> is a number that describes it: μ (mean), σ (standard deviation), p (proportion). A parameter is <b>fixed</b> and usually <b>unknown</b>.<ex>All bearings this machine produces; μ = their mean diameter.</ex>",
  s:"The population is everything you care about. A parameter is the true number for all of it. You almost never get to see it, but it is one fixed number.<ex>The true average wait time of every call a call center will ever get. Nobody knows it exactly, but it exists.</ex>"},
 {f:"Sample and statistic", b:"A <b>sample</b> is the subset we actually observe. A <b>statistic</b> is a number computed from the sample: x̄, s, p̂. We compute statistics to learn about parameters.<ex>64 bearings measured: their average x̄ is a statistic that estimates μ.</ex>",
  s:"A sample is the part you actually look at. A statistic is the number you calculate from it, and you use it as your best guess for the parameter.<ex>You time 50 calls and get an average of 4.2 minutes. 4.2 is a statistic.</ex>"},
 {f:"Parameter or statistic?", b:"Ask: is it about <b>every unit</b> (parameter) or about <b>the units we measured</b> (statistic)?<ex>\"The average balance of all checking accounts\" → parameter μ. \"The 200 sampled accounts average $2,140\" → statistic x̄ = 2,140.</ex>",
  s:"If you could only know it by checking everyone, it's a parameter. If you got it from your data, it's a statistic.<ex>\"38% of the 108 drives were hooks\" is a statistic. \"The true share of all his drives that are hooks\" is the parameter p.</ex>"},
 {f:"Random sample (this course's meaning)", b:"Every observation comes from the <b>same population</b> and is <b>independent</b> of the others. That is what makes the sampling-distribution formulas work. A big sample that was <b>not</b> collected randomly does not become random by being big.",
  s:"Random sample = each item is picked fairly and one pick doesn't affect another. Size can't fix a bad way of picking.<ex>5,000 people who chose to answer an online survey are not a random sample of your customers, even though 5,000 is a lot.</ex>"},
 {f:"A statistic is a random variable", b:"Before the sample is collected we don't know which units it will contain, so the sample mean is a <b>random variable</b>, written <b>X̄</b>. The number from the sample we actually got, <b>x̄</b>, is one <b>realization</b> of it. Capital = random variable, lowercase = its value.<ex>X̄ = the mean of a future random sample of 64 bearings; x̄ = .5003 is what our sample gave.</ex>",
  s:"Before you sample, the average you'll get is unknown. It could come out a bit high or a bit low. So it behaves like a random number. After you sample, you get one actual value.<ex>Roll a die 10 times and average the rolls. Before rolling, that average could be 3.1 or 3.9 or anything in between. That's X̄. The 3.4 you actually get is x̄.</ex>"},
 {f:"Sampling distribution", b:"The probability distribution of a statistic: a distribution <b>over all possible random samples of the same size n</b> from the same population. It describes how the statistic would vary from sample to sample. We never see it directly (we only collect one sample); we know its shape from theory.[[clt:skew|n=64|cap=Left: the population of individual units. Right: the sampling distribution of x̄ for n = 64, narrower and normal]]",
  s:"Imagine taking a sample of the same size over and over, thousands of times, and computing the average each time. The pattern those averages make is the sampling distribution.<ex>Sample 64 bottles and average their fill, then repeat that a thousand times. The 1,000 averages pile up in a narrow bell around the true mean.</ex>"},
 {f:"n versus the number of samples", b:"<b>n</b> is the size of <b>one</b> sample. The sampling distribution concerns the many <b>hypothetical</b> samples of that size we could have drawn. Don't describe it as the distribution of the data in your sample.<ex>n = 64 bearings. The sampling distribution is over all the different groups of 64 we might have picked.</ex>",
  s:"n is how many items are in your one sample. The \"many samples\" are imaginary repeats you use to think about how much your average could have come out differently.<ex>One sample of 64 bearings, so n = 64, not \"1 sample\".</ex>"},
 {f:"\"If I only collect one sample, what is the sampling distribution of?\"", b:"Of the statistic, over all the samples of the same size you <b>could</b> have drawn. You never see the others, but theory says how the statistic would vary across them, and that tells you how far your one x̄ is likely to be from μ. Your sample is one draw from that distribution.",
  s:"It's the spread of all the averages you could have gotten. Your one sample is a single draw from it. That's what lets you say how far off your average might be.<ex>Like one ticket from a raffle drum: you know what's in the drum even though you only pulled one.</ex>"}
]},
{id:"se", title:"Standard error and the CLT", sub:"How sample means behave", cards:[
 {f:"Standard error of the mean", b:"The standard deviation of the sampling distribution of x̄: <b>σ/√n</b>. It measures how far x̄ typically falls from μ across repeated samples of size n.<ex>σ = .001, n = 64 → SE = .001/√64 = .001/8 = .000125</ex>[[normal:0,1|btw=-1,1|lab=about 68% within 1 SE|cap=The sampling distribution of x̄ is centered at μ with spread SE = σ/√n]]",
  s:"Standard error = how much your sample average usually misses the true average by. Bigger samples miss by less.<ex>Packages weigh 500 g give or take 12 g. Average 36 of them: the average is off by only about 12/6 = 2 g.</ex>"},
 {f:"Standard deviation vs standard error", b:"<b>σ</b> describes how individual units vary around μ (one bearing vs another). <b>σ/√n</b> describes how sample <b>means</b> vary around μ (one sample's average vs another's). Averages vary less than individuals, and less the more units go into each average.",
  s:"SD is about single items. SE is about averages. Averages are calmer than single items because highs and lows cancel out.<ex>One person's height varies a lot. The average height of 100 people barely changes from group to group.</ex>"},
 {f:"Sample size and the standard error", b:"SE shrinks with n, but only like <b>1/√n</b>: <b>four times</b> the sample size <b>halves</b> the standard error.<ex>σ = 12: n = 36 → SE = 2; n = 144 → SE = 1.</ex>",
  s:"To cut the error in half you need four times as much data, not twice as much.<ex>36 customers gives an error of 2; you need 144 customers to get it down to 1.</ex>"},
 {f:"Unbiased estimator", b:"An estimator is <b>unbiased</b> when the expected value of its sampling distribution equals the parameter. x̄ is unbiased for μ: averaged over all possible samples, it's exactly right. Unbiasedness is about the <b>center</b>; the standard error is about the <b>spread</b>.",
  s:"Unbiased means it doesn't lean high or low on average. Any single sample can still be off, but there's no systematic tilt.<ex>A fair scale might read a little high or low each time, but on average it's right. That's unbiased.</ex>"},
 {f:"The Central Limit Theorem", b:"For a <b>random sample</b> of size n from a population with mean μ and SD σ, the sampling distribution of x̄ is <b>approximately normal</b> with mean μ and SD σ/√n when n is large enough, <b>whatever the shape of the population</b>. If the population itself is normal, x̄ is normal for any n.<ex>X̄ ~ N(μ, σ/√n) approximately (on the formula sheet).</ex>[[clt:skew|n=36|cap=A skewed population (left) still gives a bell-shaped sampling distribution of x̄ (right)]]",
  s:"Even if individual values are lopsided, averages of a big enough random sample come out in a bell shape. That bell is what lets you use the z table.<ex>Delivery times are skewed (a few very late ones), but the average of 50 random deliveries follows a bell curve.</ex>"},
 {f:"CLT conditions, checked with numbers", b:"Name the result and check it against the problem: a <b>random sample</b>, and <b>n ≥ 30</b> (rule of thumb) <b>or</b> an approximately normal population. Then state the distribution.<ex>\"By the CLT, since the sample is random and n = 64 ≥ 30, X̄ ~ N(μ, σ/√n) approximately.\"</ex>",
  s:"Before using the bell curve, say why you're allowed to: it's random, and it's big enough (30+) or the population is already bell-shaped. Put the actual number in.<ex>Write \"n = 64 ≥ 30\", not just \"n is large\".</ex>"},
 {f:"n ≥ 30 is a rule of thumb, not a law", b:"The theorem says the approximation improves as n grows; how fast depends on the population. Close to normal: small n is fine. <b>Very skewed</b>: 30 may not be enough. Errors in both directions are common: assuming n ≥ 30 guarantees normality for an extremely skewed population, or assuming n &lt; 30 rules out the t-test when the population is approximately normal.",
  s:"30 is a guideline. Very lopsided data needs more; nicely shaped data needs less.<ex>Invoice amounts (a few huge ones) can need way more than 30. Heights, which are already bell-shaped, are fine with 10.</ex>"},
 {f:"Somewhat skewed, extremely skewed, small n", b:"• <b>Somewhat skewed, n large</b> (n = 64): the answer is still valid; the CLT handles moderate skew.<br>• <b>Extremely skewed, n large</b>: may still be acceptable, but needs more caution because the rule of thumb is least reliable for strongly skewed populations; check the shape of the sample data.<br>• <b>Strongly skewed, n small</b> (9 or 16): not reliable; the population isn't normal and n is too small for the CLT.",
  s:"Big sample + mild lopsidedness = fine. Big sample + very lopsided = probably okay, but be careful and say why. Small sample + lopsided = don't trust the bell curve.<ex>16 invoice amounts from a very skewed population: the average won't be bell-shaped yet.</ex>"},
 {f:"A large sample doesn't fix a biased sample", b:"The CLT concerns <b>sampling variability</b> in a <b>random</b> sample. It says nothing about <b>selection bias</b>. A self-selected or convenience sample stays biased however large it is.<ex>5,000 website survey responses: n is huge, but the people who chose to answer may differ from all customers.</ex>",
  s:"A huge pile of the wrong people still gives the wrong answer. Size helps with luck, not with bias.<ex>Asking only gym members how often people exercise. Ask 10,000 of them and the answer is still too high.</ex>"},
 {f:"Law of Large Numbers vs CLT", b:"<b>LLN:</b> as n grows, x̄ gets closer and closer to μ (where it goes).<br><b>CLT:</b> for a given n, the sampling distribution of x̄ is approximately normal around μ with SE σ/√n (what shape it has). The CLT is the one that lets us compute probabilities.",
  s:"LLN: more data gets you closer to the truth. CLT: tells you the bell shape of the averages, so you can calculate how likely a certain miss is.<ex>Flip a coin 10,000 times and the share of heads ends up near 50% (LLN). The CLT tells you how likely 52% or more is.</ex>"}
]},
{id:"t1", title:"Template 1: probabilities for x̄", sub:"Within a distance, above, below, decision rules", cards:[
 {f:"\"Within d of the population mean\"", b:"Write it as an expression first: P(μ − d ≤ X̄ ≤ μ + d). Standardize with the <b>standard error</b>: z = d/(σ/√n). Then P(−z ≤ Z ≤ z) = 2·(table value − .5).<ex>d = .0001, SE = .000125 → z = 0.8 → 2(.7881 − .5) = .5762</ex>[[normal:0,1|btw=-0.8,0.8|cap=P(−0.8 ≤ Z ≤ 0.8) = 2(.7881 − .5) = .5762]]",
  s:"\"Within d\" means the average lands in a band around the truth. Turn d into a z by dividing by the standard error, then take the middle area of the bell.<ex>Band of ±2 g with SE = 2 g → z = 1 → about 68% of sample averages land in it.</ex>"},
 {f:"Why 2·(table − .5)?", b:"The table gives the area to the <b>left</b> of z. Area between 0 and z = table − .5. By <b>symmetry</b> the area between −z and 0 is the same, so the middle is twice that. Equivalently: table(z) − (1 − table(z)).",
  s:"The table counts from the far left. Take away the left half (0.5) to get from the middle to z, then double it for both sides.<ex>table(1.00) = .8413 → .3413 on each side → .6826 in the middle.</ex>"},
 {f:"Above or below a value", b:"P(X̄ > a) = 1 − P(Z ≤ (a − μ)/SE). P(X̄ &lt; a) = P(Z &lt; (a − μ)/SE), using symmetry for negative z: P(Z &lt; −z) = 1 − P(Z &lt; z).[[normal:500,2|gt=503|cap=P(X̄ > 503) when μ = 500, SE = 2: z = 1.5, 1 − .9332 = .0668]]",
  s:"Convert the value to a z with the standard error, read the table, and flip it (1 minus) if you want the right side.<ex>Average above 503 g when the mean is 500 and SE is 2: z = 1.5, so 1 − .9332 = .0668.</ex>"},
 {f:"Use the SE, not σ", b:"z = (x̄ − μ)/(σ/√n). Using σ alone in the denominator is the most common error on this template: it treats the average as if it varied like one unit.<ex>σ = .001, n = 64: z = .0001/.000125 = 0.8, not .0001/.001 = 0.1.</ex>",
  s:"You're asking about an average, so divide by the average's error (σ/√n), not by σ itself.<ex>Dividing by σ makes averages look way more spread out than they really are.</ex>"},
 {f:"A decision rule with two limits", b:"\"Out of control if x̄ &lt; L or x̄ > U\": P(X̄ &lt; L <b>or</b> X̄ > U) = P(X̄ &lt; L) + P(X̄ > U), because the two events are <b>mutually exclusive</b> (addition rule). Compute both tails at the <b>true</b> mean.[[normal:0,1|out=-12.8,-3.2|cap=At the shifted mean both limits sit far to the left: one tail is ≈ 0, the other is nearly everything]]",
  s:"\"Below the low limit OR above the high limit\" can't both happen at once, so add the two chances.<ex>2% chance of too low + 3% chance of too high = 5% chance the rule goes off.</ex>"},
 {f:"When one tail is essentially zero", b:"After a big shift, one z is far out (like −12.8): that tail is ≈ 0. Say so and add it anyway, showing both terms.<ex>P(Z &lt; −12.8) ≈ 0, P(Z > −3.2) = P(Z ≤ 3.2) ≈ .9993 → total ≈ .9993.</ex>",
  s:"If a z is past ±4, its tail is basically zero. Write ≈ 0 and still show you checked it.<ex>z = −12.8 is so far out that nothing is there.</ex>"},
 {f:"Beyond the end of the table", b:"The table ends at z = 3.09 (area .9990). For z = 3.2: P(Z ≤ 3.2) ≈ .9993, and from the table you can say it's <b>at least .9990</b>.",
  s:"If the z is off the table, the area to the left is above .9990. Just say that.<ex>P(Z &lt; 3.5) is over .9990.</ex>"},
 {f:"Part (c) is a power question", b:"The probability that the rule signals \"out of control\" when the mean <b>has</b> shifted is the <b>power</b> of the rule at that shift. Its complement is the probability of a <b>Type II error</b> (missing the shift). Power rises with a bigger shift, a bigger n (smaller SE), a bigger α, a smaller σ.",
  s:"Power = the chance your alarm actually goes off when something really is wrong. More data and bigger problems make it go off more reliably.<ex>A smoke detector that beeps 99.9% of the time when there's real smoke has high power.</ex>"},
 {f:"False alarms: the rule when nothing changed", b:"If the mean has <b>not</b> shifted, the probability the rule signals anyway is its <b>Type I error</b> rate (false-alarm rate). With limits at μ ± k·SE it is 2·(1 − table(k)).<ex>Limits at μ ± 3 SE: 2(1 − .9987) = .0026.</ex>[[normal:0,1|out=-3,3|cap=Both tails beyond ±3: 2(1 − .9987) = .0026, the false-alarm rate]]",
  s:"Even a perfect machine sometimes produces an unusual average. If that trips the alarm, it's a false alarm.<ex>About 1 in 385 samples from a perfectly fine process lands outside 3-SE limits.</ex>"},
 {f:"The rule is an x-bar control chart", b:"Center line μ, control limits <b>μ ± 3·σ/√n</b>. Each sample checked against the limits is a hypothesis test of \"the process is in control\". A point outside signals a possible <b>assignable cause</b> (worn tool, changed setting), not proof.[[cc:0|cap=An x-bar chart: center line, control limits at μ ± 3σ/√n, one point signaling]]",
  s:"A control chart is just the decision rule drawn as a picture over time: a middle line and two fences. A dot outside a fence means go check the machine.<ex>Fill weights: center 500 g, fences at 494 and 506 g when σ = 4 and n = 4.</ex>"}
]},
{id:"prop", title:"Template 2: the sample proportion", sub:"Its sampling distribution and conditions", cards:[
 {f:"p versus p̂", b:"<b>p</b> = the true proportion in the population (a fixed number we don't know). <b>p̂</b> = x/n from the sample (a realization of a random variable: a different random sample would give a different p̂).<ex>41 hooks out of 108 drives: p̂ = 41/108 ≈ .38; p = the true share of all of Adam's drives that are hooks.</ex>",
  s:"p is the real rate. p̂ is the rate in your sample. Different samples give different p̂'s around p.<ex>The true defect rate of a factory is p. You check 200 parts and find 12 bad: p̂ = .06.</ex>"},
 {f:"Sampling distribution of p̂", b:"By the CLT, for a random sample with enough successes and failures: <b>p̂ ~ N(p, √(p(1 − p)/n))</b> approximately (on the formula sheet). p̂ summarizes a count of successes in independent trials with constant probability, the binomial structure.",
  s:"Proportions from big random samples also follow a bell curve around the true rate.<ex>Share of hooks in 108 drives: bell-shaped around his true hook rate.</ex>"},
 {f:"Conditions for a proportion (not on the sheet)", b:"A <b>random sample</b> with independent outcomes, and the course's rule of thumb: <b>n ≥ 30, n·p̂ ≥ 15, n(1 − p̂) ≥ 15</b>. Check with numbers: n·p̂ is the number of successes, n(1 − p̂) the number of failures.<ex>n·p̂ = 41 ≥ 15 and n(1 − p̂) = 108 − 41 = 67 ≥ 15.</ex>",
  s:"You need at least 15 \"yes\" results and 15 \"no\" results in the sample. Count them and write the numbers.<ex>4 successes out of 40: too few, so no bell curve.</ex>"},
 {f:"Too few successes", b:"If n·p̂ or n(1 − p̂) is under 15, the normal approximation is not justified, even when n ≥ 30. Say so.<ex>40 trials, 4 successes: n·p̂ = 4 &lt; 15. Not enough successes for the normal model.</ex>",
  s:"Rare events need bigger samples. A handful of successes isn't enough to trust the bell curve.<ex>2 complaints out of 100 orders: too few to use the normal model.</ex>"},
 {f:"Standard error of p̂", b:"σ<sub>p̂</sub> = √(p(1 − p)/n). We don't know p, so for an interval we <b>estimate</b> it with p̂: √(p̂(1 − p̂)/n). Do the division and the square root in visible steps.<ex>√(.38 × .62/108) = √(.2356/108) = √.002181 ≈ .047</ex>",
  s:"Plug your sample rate into the formula: rate × (1 − rate), divide by n, square root.<ex>.38 × .62 = .2356; ÷ 108 = .00218; √ = .047.</ex>"},
 {f:"Why z (not t) for a proportion?", b:"For a proportion the standard error depends only on p, so there's no separate σ to estimate: we use the normal (z), not Student's t, which is for a sample mean when we must use s.",
  s:"For proportions you always use the z table. The t table is for averages when you only have the sample SD.<ex>Conversion rate interval → z. Average order value with s → t.</ex>"},
 {f:"Point vs interval estimator", b:"A <b>point estimator</b> gives one number (p̂ for p, x̄ for μ). An <b>interval estimator</b> gives a range of values, built so that a stated share of such intervals, over repeated samples, contain the parameter.<ex>p̂ = .38 is the point estimate; (.303, .457) is the interval estimate.</ex>",
  s:"Point = single best guess. Interval = a range that admits you might be off.<ex>\"About 38%\" vs \"somewhere from 30% to 46%\".</ex>"}
]},
{id:"ci", title:"Confidence intervals", sub:"Build them, read them, judge claims", cards:[
 {f:"CI for a proportion: the steps", b:"Formula: p̂ ± z<sub>α/2</sub>·√(p̂(1 − p̂)/n).<br>1. α = 1 − CL, α/2.<br>2. Critical value: right tail α/2, so look up <b>1 − α/2</b> in the table.<br>3. Standard error.<br>4. Margin = critical value × SE; both limits.<br>5. A sentence in context.[[ci:.303,.457,.38|cap=The 90% interval for the hook rate: .38 ± 1.64(.047)]]",
  s:"Center ± (z × standard error). The z comes from your confidence level. Finish with a sentence saying what the range is about.<ex>.38 ± 1.64 × .047 = .303 to .457.</ex>"},
 {f:"Finding z<sub>α/2</sub> in the table", b:"The table gives area to the <b>left</b>, so look up <b>1 − α/2</b>:<br>• 90%: α/2 = .05 → .95 → between .9495 (1.64) and .9505 (1.65): <b>1.64 or 1.65</b><br>• 95%: α/2 = .025 → .975 → exactly <b>1.96</b><br>• 99%: α/2 = .005 → .995 → between .9949 (2.57) and .9951 (2.58): <b>2.57 or 2.58</b><br>Say which you used.[[normal:0,1|out=-1.64,1.64|lab=.05 each tail|cap=90% in the middle leaves .05 in each tail, so z = 1.64]]",
  s:"Split what's left over between the two tails, then find the z that leaves that much on the right.<ex>95% sure → 5% left over → 2.5% each side → z = 1.96.</ex>"},
 {f:"The tail-area trap", b:"For 90% confidence the right-tail area is <b>.05</b>, not .10. Looking up .90 gives 1.28, which builds an 80% interval. A common error.",
  s:"90% confidence means 10% total in the tails, but split in two: 5% on each side.<ex>Using 1.28 instead of 1.64 makes the interval too narrow.</ex>"},
 {f:"Margin of error (memorize)", b:"Margin of error = <b>critical value × standard error</b>. The interval is estimate ± margin.<ex>1.64 × .047 ≈ .077 → .38 ± .077.</ex>",
  s:"How far the interval reaches on each side of your estimate.<ex>\"38% ± 7.7 points\".</ex>"},
 {f:"Report the interval in context", b:"End with a sentence about the <b>parameter</b>, in the problem's language: \"We are 90% confident that the true proportion of hooks is between .303 and .457.\"",
  s:"Say what the numbers mean for the real situation, using the true rate (not your sample).<ex>\"We're 95% confident the true on-time rate is between 82% and 89%.\"</ex>"},
 {f:"What \"90% confident\" means (full credit)", b:"A statement about the <b>procedure</b>: if we repeated the sampling many times, with samples of the same size, and built an interval the same way each time, about <b>90% of those intervals would contain the true parameter</b> and 10% would not.[[cis:20,90|cap=20 intervals from 20 different samples: most cover the true value (line); a couple miss]]",
  s:"The 90% is about the method's track record, not about this one interval. Do it 100 times and about 90 of the intervals catch the truth.<ex>Like a basketball player who makes 90% of free throws: that's about her shooting, not about one particular shot after it's already gone in or missed.</ex>"},
 {f:"Interpretation: what loses points", b:"<b>Partial:</b> \"We are 90% confident p is between .303 and .457\" offered as the meaning of 90% (it restates rather than explains).<br><b>No credit:</b> \"90% probability that p is in the interval\" (p is fixed, not random) · \"90% of drives are between .303 and .457\" (confuses with individual outcomes) · \"90% of samples have p̂ in the interval\" (confuses with the sampling distribution of p̂).",
  s:"Never say \"90% chance the true value is in my interval\". Once you've built it, the true value is either in it or not; the 90% describes the method.<ex>Right: \"90% of intervals made this way would contain p.\"</ex>"},
 {f:"Why not \"90% probability\"?", b:"The true parameter is a fixed number. <b>Before</b> sampling, the interval was random and the procedure had a 90% chance of producing one that contains p. <b>After</b>, your interval either contains p or doesn't; you just don't know which.",
  s:"The truth doesn't move. Your interval either caught it or it didn't. The 90% was the chance before you drew the sample.<ex>A coin already flipped under your hand isn't 50% heads anymore; it's one or the other.</ex>"},
 {f:"Judging a claim with an interval", b:"• Claim <b>inside</b> the interval: compatible with the data, <b>not confirmed</b>; other values are compatible too.<br>• Claim <b>outside</b>: not supported at that confidence level.<ex>Adam says 45%: .45 is inside (.303, .457), so it doesn't contradict the data, but .35 and .40 are compatible too.</ex>[[ci:.303,.457,.38|claim=.45|cap=The claim .45 falls inside: plausible, not proven]]",
  s:"Inside the range = could be right, but so could other values. Outside = the data don't back it up.<ex>\"Half our customers are repeat buyers\" with an interval of 31% to 42%: the data don't support 50%.</ex>"},
 {f:"Higher confidence, wider interval", b:"Raising the confidence level (90% → 99%) increases the critical value, so the margin grows and the interval gets <b>wider</b>. A larger n makes it <b>narrower</b> (smaller SE).",
  s:"To be more sure you catch the truth, you have to cast a wider net.<ex>99% interval: 2.58 × SE. 90% interval: 1.64 × SE.</ex>"},
 {f:"CI for a mean: σ known vs s", b:"σ known: <b>x̄ ± z<sub>α/2</sub>·σ/√n</b>.<br>σ unknown (estimated by s): <b>x̄ ± t<sub>α/2</sub>·s/√n</b> with <b>n − 1</b> degrees of freedom (t table, column α/2).<ex>n = 16, x̄ = 42, s = 8, 95%: t<sub>.025,15</sub> = 2.131 → 42 ± 2.131(2) = 42 ± 4.262.</ex>",
  s:"Know the true SD? Use z. Only have the sample's SD? Use t with n − 1.<ex>Machine with a long-run known σ → z. Ten customers' spending with s → t, 9 d.f.</ex>"},
 {f:"Sample size for a margin of error", b:"For a mean with σ known: n = (z<sub>α/2</sub>·σ/E)², rounded <b>up</b>. For a proportion: n = z²·p(1 − p)/E² (use .5 if p is unknown).<ex>σ = 10, E = 2, 95%: (1.96 × 10/2)² = 96.04 → 97.</ex>",
  s:"Work backward from how precise you want to be. Always round up.<ex>Want ±2 with σ = 10 at 95%: need 97 people.</ex>"}
]},
{id:"test", title:"Template 3: hypothesis tests", sub:"Hypotheses, t, rejection region, p-value", cards:[
 {f:"Null and alternative", b:"<b>H0</b>: the status quo or no effect; always contains the <b>equality</b> (μ = 1).<br><b>HA</b>: what the research is trying to establish (μ > 1).<br>Both are about the <b>parameter</b> (μ, p), never x̄ or p̂.<ex>\"Is the average tech stock riskier than the market?\" H0: μ = 1, HA: μ > 1.</ex>",
  s:"H0 = nothing special is going on. HA = the thing you're trying to show. Write them about the true average, not your sample.<ex>\"Deliveries take longer than the promised 30 minutes\": H0: μ = 30, HA: μ > 30.</ex>"},
 {f:"Why the claim goes in HA", b:"A test can only produce evidence <b>against</b> H0; it can never confirm H0. So the claim we want evidence for goes in the alternative, where rejecting H0 can support it.",
  s:"Tests work like court: you can prove guilt, not innocence. Put the thing you want to prove in HA.<ex>To show a new process is faster, HA says it's faster.</ex>"},
 {f:"Direction of the test", b:"Match HA to the question: \"riskier / longer / more than\" → <b>right-tailed</b> (>); \"less / reduces / below\" → <b>left-tailed</b> (&lt;); \"different from / changed\" → <b>two-tailed</b> (≠). Say which tail you use.",
  s:"Read the claim's direction: more → right tail, less → left tail, just \"different\" → both tails.<ex>\"Customers wait less than 5 minutes\" → HA: μ &lt; 5, left tail.</ex>"},
 {f:"z or t?", b:"<b>σ known</b> (a process measured over a long time) → <b>z</b>. <b>σ unknown</b>, estimated by s → <b>t</b> with <b>n − 1</b> d.f. For a <b>proportion</b> → z (its SE depends only on p).<ex>Betas: only s = .37 is given, σ unknown → cannot use z; use t with 14 d.f.</ex>",
  s:"Ask: do I know the true SD? Yes → z. Only the sample's s → t.<ex>\"The long-run SD is 4 g\" → z. \"The sample SD was 4 g\" → t.</ex>"},
 {f:"t vs normal", b:"With s in place of σ, (x̄ − μ)/(s/√n) follows a <b>t-distribution with n − 1 d.f.</b> It's symmetric like the normal but has <b>heavier tails</b>, so its critical values are <b>larger</b>. As d.f. grow it approaches the normal (last row of the t table = z values).[[tvz:4|cap=t with 4 d.f. (heavier tails) vs the standard normal]]",
  s:"t is a slightly fatter bell that accounts for not knowing the true SD. With lots of data it turns into the normal.<ex>95% two-sided: t with 4 d.f. needs 2.776; z needs only 1.96.</ex>"},
 {f:"Assumptions of the t-test", b:"A <b>random sample</b>, <b>and</b> either n ≥ 30 <b>or</b> an approximately normal population. With a small sample, say the test relies on the population being approximately normal.<ex>n = 15 &lt; 30, so the betas must be approximately normally distributed.</ex>",
  s:"Random sample, plus either a big sample or bell-shaped data. Small sample? You're counting on bell-shaped data.<ex>12 customers: the test is only okay if spending is roughly bell-shaped.</ex>"},
 {f:"Degrees of freedom", b:"For a one-sample t: <b>d.f. = n − 1</b>. Using n is a common error.<ex>n = 15 → 14 d.f.</ex>",
  s:"Always one less than the sample size.<ex>20 observations → 19 d.f.</ex>"},
 {f:"Rejection region", b:"State the tail area, the d.f., the critical value from the t table, and the rule as an inequality:<br>• Right: P(T > t) = α → reject if <b>t > t<sub>α</sub></b><br>• Left: reject if <b>t &lt; −t<sub>α</sub></b><br>• Two-tailed: α/2 in each tail → reject if <b>|t| > t<sub>α/2</sub></b><ex>α = .1, 14 d.f., right-tailed: t<sub>.1</sub> = 1.345 → reject H0 if t > 1.345.</ex>[[tdist:14|gt=1.345|cap=Right-tailed, α = .10, 14 d.f.: reject when t > 1.345]]",
  s:"Find the cutoff in the t table (row = d.f., column = α, or α/2 for two-sided). Beyond the cutoff = reject.<ex>Left-tailed at .05 with 9 d.f.: reject if t &lt; −1.833.</ex>"},
 {f:"The test statistic", b:"t = (x̄ − μ<sub>0</sub>)/(s/√n). Show the substitution and the SE.<ex>(1.23 − 1)/(.37/√15) = .23/.0955 ≈ 2.408</ex>",
  s:"How many standard errors your sample average is from the claimed value.<ex>Average 1.23 vs claim 1, SE .0955 → 2.4 SEs above.</ex>"},
 {f:"A four-part conclusion", b:"1. The <b>comparison</b> (2.408 > 1.345)<br>2. The <b>decision</b> (reject H0 / fail to reject H0)<br>3. The <b>significance level</b> (at the 10% level)<br>4. A sentence in <b>context</b> about the population.<ex>\"Since 2.408 > 1.345, we reject H0. At the 10% significance level, there is enough evidence to conclude that the average high-technology stock is riskier than the overall market.\"</ex>",
  s:"Compare, decide, name the level, say what it means in real life.<ex>\"1.2 &lt; 1.729, so we fail to reject H0 at 5%: not enough evidence that deliveries average more than 30 minutes.\"</ex>"},
 {f:"Conclusions that lose points", b:"<b>Partial:</b> the right decision with no comparison, no α, or no context.<br><b>No credit:</b> \"accept H0\" (a test can't show H0 is true) · \"this proves...\" (tests control error rates; they don't prove) · a statement about the <b>sample</b> (\"x̄ is greater than 1\": we knew that before testing).",
  s:"Never \"accept H0\" and never \"proves\". Talk about the population, not your sample.<ex>Not \"the sample average was higher\" but \"the average for all stocks is higher\".</ex>"},
 {f:"Failing to reject is not accepting", b:"It means the data are not inconsistent with H0. They're usually consistent with many other values too, especially with a small sample. Write: \"there is not enough evidence to conclude [the alternative]\".",
  s:"\"Not guilty\" isn't \"innocent\". You just didn't find enough evidence.<ex>Failing to show deliveries are late doesn't prove they're on time.</ex>"},
 {f:"p-value: definition", b:"The probability, <b>computed assuming H0 is true</b>, of a test statistic at least as extreme (in the direction of HA) as the one observed. Roughly: how surprising the data would be if H0 were true. Equivalently, the smallest α at which we'd reject H0.",
  s:"If nothing special were going on, how often would you see data this extreme? Small p = very surprising = evidence against H0.<ex>p = .02: if the claim were false, results like ours would show up only 2% of the time.</ex>"},
 {f:"Bracketing a p-value from the t table", b:"Find where |t| falls in its d.f. row; the p-value lies between those columns' tail areas. Bigger t → smaller tail area.<ex>14 d.f., t = 2.408: P(T > 2.145) = .025 and P(T > 2.624) = .01 → .01 &lt; p-value &lt; .025.</ex>Two-tailed: double both ends.[[tt:14,2.408|cap=Row 14: 2.408 sits between 2.145 (.025) and 2.624 (.01)]]",
  s:"Find your t between two numbers in its row; the p-value is between those two column headings.<ex>t = 2.0 with 10 d.f. is between 1.812 (.05) and 2.228 (.025), so p is between .025 and .05.</ex>"},
 {f:"Interpreting the p-value (full credit)", b:"Either one earns full credit:<br>• \"If the true mean were μ<sub>0</sub> (H0 true), the probability of a t-statistic of 2.408 or larger would be between .01 and .025.\"<br>• \"We would reject H0 for any α larger than .025 (the upper bound of the p-value).\"<br><b>Never</b>: the probability that H0 is true, that the result is due to chance, or of making a mistake.",
  s:"Say it assuming H0 is true, or say which α levels would reject. Don't call it the chance H0 is true.<ex>\"For any α above .025 we'd reject H0.\"</ex>"},
 {f:"Type I and Type II errors", b:"<b>Type I:</b> reject H0 when it's true; probability = α (we choose it).<br><b>Type II:</b> fail to reject H0 when it's false.<br>Describe each <b>in the language of the problem</b>. For fixed n, lowering α makes a Type II error more likely.<ex>Type I: concluding tech stocks are riskier when they're not. Type II: failing to detect that they are.</ex>",
  s:"Type I = false alarm. Type II = missed it. Making false alarms rarer makes misses more common, unless you get more data.<ex>Flagging a good batch as bad (Type I) vs shipping a bad batch (Type II).</ex>"},
 {f:"Power", b:"The probability of rejecting H0 when it's false, at a particular true value. Power = 1 − P(Type II). It increases with a <b>true value farther from H0</b>, a <b>larger n</b> (smaller SE), a <b>larger α</b>, a <b>smaller σ</b>.",
  s:"Power = how good your test is at catching a real effect. Bigger effects and more data make catching easier.<ex>Detecting a 10-minute delay is easier than a 1-minute delay.</ex>"},
 {f:"Statistical vs practical significance", b:"Statistically significant = the data are unlikely under H0. It can describe a difference far too small to matter, especially with a large sample. Whether it matters is a separate question about its size in context.<ex>200,000 customers, +$0.04 order value, p = .001: significant, probably irrelevant.</ex>",
  s:"\"Significant\" in statistics means \"real\", not \"big\". A tiny effect can be real but not worth caring about.<ex>A diet that reliably makes you lose 0.1 lb.</ex>"},
 {f:"p-hacking", b:"Trying many analyses or subsets and reporting the one with p &lt; .05 manufactures a \"significant\" result: with 20 tries at α = .05, about one comes out significant by chance alone. Same reason a control chart produces false alarms.",
  s:"Test enough things and something will look significant by luck.<ex>Checking 20 different ad colors and bragging about the one that \"won\".</ex>"}
]},
{id:"qc", title:"Quality control", sub:"Control charts, capability, frameworks", cards:[
 {f:"Common vs assignable causes", b:"<b>Common-cause</b> variation: the many small sources built into the process (slight material or temperature differences); always present, stable. <b>Assignable (special) cause</b>: a specific, identifiable change: a worn tool, a new supplier, an untrained operator.<ex>Fill volumes vary slightly with pressure (common); a worn nozzle causes a sudden drop (assignable).</ex>",
  s:"Common causes = everyday wobble. Assignable causes = something specific broke or changed.<ex>Coffee shop drinks vary a bit (common); a new barista who measures wrong (assignable).</ex>"},
 {f:"In statistical control", b:"Only common-cause variation is present, so output is <b>predictable within limits</b>. In control ≠ meeting specifications: a stable process can be consistently bad.",
  s:"In control = predictable, not necessarily good.<ex>A bakery that always burns its cookies the same amount is \"in control\".</ex>"},
 {f:"x-bar chart limits (memorize)", b:"Center line <b>μ</b>; control limits <b>μ ± 3·σ/√n</b> (three standard errors).<ex>μ = 500 g, σ = 4 g, n = 4: SE = 2 → limits 494 and 506.</ex>[[cc:0|cap=Center 500, limits 494 and 506]]",
  s:"Middle line at the target, fences 3 standard errors above and below.<ex>Target 500, SE 2 → fences at 494 and 506.</ex>"},
 {f:"p chart limits (memorize)", b:"Center line <b>p̄</b>; limits <b>p̄ ± 3·√(p̄(1 − p̄)/n)</b>. A negative lower limit is set to <b>0</b> because a proportion cannot be negative.<ex>p̄ = .02, n = 100: SE = .014 → .02 − .042 &lt; 0 → LCL = 0, UCL = .062.</ex>",
  s:"Same idea for defect rates. If the lower fence comes out negative, use 0: you can't have less than 0% defects.<ex>2% defects, samples of 100: fences at 0% and 6.2%.</ex>"},
 {f:"A control chart is a sequence of tests", b:"Each sample tests H0: in control vs HA: shifted. Outside the limits → reject. A <b>false alarm</b> (outside when in control) is a <b>Type I error</b>; missing a real shift is a <b>Type II error</b>; the chance of signaling after a shift is the <b>power</b>.",
  s:"Every new dot is a mini hypothesis test. False alarms and misses are the same two errors as before.<ex>A dot outside when nothing changed = false alarm (Type I).</ex>"},
 {f:"False alarms are certain eventually", b:"With 3-sigma limits, each in-control sample has a false-alarm probability ≈ 2(1 − .9987) = <b>.0026</b>, about <b>1 in 385</b>. Over many samples, false alarms will happen: 500 samples → about 1.3 expected.",
  s:"Check often enough and a perfectly good process will eventually set off the alarm by chance.<ex>Daily checks for a year: expect about one false alarm.</ex>"},
 {f:"A point outside doesn't prove anything", b:"It's evidence of a possible assignable cause, a reason to <b>investigate</b>. And a point inside doesn't prove nothing changed: a small shift can go undetected for many samples (Type II).",
  s:"Outside = go look. Inside = probably fine, but small problems can hide.<ex>One heavy average: check the filler, don't assume it's broken.</ex>"},
 {f:"Process capability", b:"<b>Cp = (USL − LSL)/(6σ)</b>: specification width vs process width.<br><b>Cpk = min[(USL − μ)/(3σ), (μ − LSL)/(3σ)]</b>: also accounts for an off-center mean.<ex>Specs 490–510, μ = 502, σ = 2: (510 − 502)/6 = 1.33 and (502 − 490)/6 = 2.00 → Cpk = 1.33.</ex>",
  s:"Capability = does the process fit inside what the customer allows? Cpk checks the side the process is closer to.<ex>A car that's narrower than the garage but parked off-center: Cpk looks at the tighter side.</ex>"},
 {f:"Three-sigma and six-sigma", b:"Centered, limits 3σ away (Cp = 1): about <b>0.27%</b> outside, roughly <b>2,700 per million</b> (table: 2(1 − .9987) = .0026). Six sigma puts the nearer limit 6σ away (Cp = 2); the famous <b>3.4 per million</b> assumes the mean can drift <b>1.5σ</b>, leaving 4.5σ.",
  s:"3-sigma = about 3 bad per 1,000. Six sigma = 3.4 bad per million, allowing for some drift.<ex>Six-sigma airline baggage: 3 lost bags per million.</ex>"},
 {f:"Capability needs control first", b:"Capability is meaningful only for a process that is <b>in statistical control</b>. A capability index for an unstable process describes nothing predictable.",
  s:"Don't measure how good a process is until it's stable.<ex>Grading a car's fuel economy while the engine is misfiring.</ex>"},
 {f:"ISO 9001, Six Sigma, Deming", b:"<b>ISO 9001</b>: a standard for quality management systems; certification means processes are documented and followed, <b>not</b> that products are defect-free.<br><b>Six Sigma</b>: reduce process variation, organized around <b>DMAIC</b> (define, measure, analyze, improve, control).<br><b>Deming</b>: most variation comes from the <b>system</b>, which is management's responsibility; reacting to common-cause variation as if it had an assignable cause is <b>tampering</b> and makes a process worse.",
  s:"ISO = you follow your documented process. Six Sigma = cut variation step by step. Deming = fix the system, don't blame workers, and don't fiddle with a stable process.<ex>Adjusting a machine every time a dot is a little above the middle line: tampering.</ex>"}
]},
{id:"spot", title:"Spot the template", sub:"Recognize the structure under a new story", cards:[
 {f:"Template 1 signature", b:"A process or population with a <b>known</b> standard deviation (\"over a long period of time\"); a random sample of n; the probability that x̄ falls in a range; a question about the population's shape; a decision rule on x̄ and the probability it signals.<ex>Fill volumes, package weights, call handling times, delivery times, order values, part lengths.</ex>",
  s:"Known σ + questions about where the sample average lands = Template 1.<ex>\"Packages average 2 kg with σ = 0.3 kg; sample 36...\"</ex>"},
 {f:"Template 2 signature", b:"A <b>count of successes out of n</b>, a random sample with independent outcomes; questions about p̂'s distribution, a confidence interval, what the confidence level means, and whether a claimed value is compatible.<ex>Defect rates, conversion rates, churn, on-time deliveries, invoices paid late, offers accepted.</ex>",
  s:"\"x out of n\" + a confidence interval + someone's claim = Template 2.<ex>\"Of 250 visitors, 37 bought something...\"</ex>"},
 {f:"Template 3 signature", b:"A research claim that a mean is <b>above, below, or different from</b> a stated value; a small random sample with <b>x̄ and s</b>; questions on hypotheses, z vs t, assumptions, rejection region, conclusion, p-value.<ex>Delivery time vs a promise, fill weight vs the label, satisfaction vs a target, days to pay vs terms.</ex>",
  s:"A claim about an average + x̄ and s from a small sample = Template 3.<ex>\"A random sample of 18 tickets took 5.6 hours on average, s = 1.3; the SLA promises 5 hours...\"</ex>"},
 {f:"Near misses to watch for", b:"• A large convenience or self-selected sample (not random; size doesn't fix it)<br>• A small sample from a strongly skewed population (invoice amounts)<br>• A proportion with only a handful of successes<br>• Dependent observations (several employees from each of a few stores; repeated measures on the same customer)<br>• A claim worded to tempt you into H0<br>• Tampering: adjusting inside the limits<br>• Capability judged for a process not in control",
  s:"These look like normal problems but a condition fails. Spot it and say what breaks.<ex>\"We asked 3 employees at each of 10 stores\": those answers aren't independent.</ex>"}
]},
{id:"grading", title:"How you're graded", sub:"What a full-credit answer shows", cards:[
 {f:"1. Name the parameter in words", b:"\"μ = the population mean beta of high-technology stocks\", \"p = the true proportion of Adam's drives that are hooks\".", s:"Say in words what μ or p stands for in this problem.<ex>\"μ = average delivery time of all orders on this route\".</ex>"},
 {f:"2. Justify the normal model", b:"Name the <b>Central Limit Theorem</b> and check its conditions with numbers: a random sample, and n = 64 ≥ 30; or n·p̂ = 41 ≥ 15 and n(1 − p̂) = 67 ≥ 15. Then state the distribution: X̄ ~ N(μ, σ/√n) approximately.", s:"Write \"by the CLT\", show the condition numbers, then the distribution.<ex>\"Random, n = 49 ≥ 30, so X̄ ~ N(μ, σ/√49).\"</ex>"},
 {f:"3. Use the standard error", b:"z = (x̄ − μ)/(σ/√n), not σ.", s:"Divide by σ/√n when it's about an average.<ex>σ = 14, n = 49 → divide by 2, not 14.</ex>"},
 {f:"4. Expression → numbers → result", b:"Write every probability as an expression first, then the numbers, then the result; quote the table value and show the complement or symmetry step.", s:"P(...) first, then plug in, then the answer, with the table number you used.<ex>P(X̄ > 503) = P(Z > 1.5) = 1 − .9332 = .0668</ex>"},
 {f:"5. A complete confidence interval", b:"The formula, α and α/2 from the CL, the critical value with the table lookup explained, the SE, both limits, and a sentence in context.", s:"Formula, α, z from the table, SE, both ends, one sentence.<ex>\"...so we are 95% confident the true churn rate is between .11 and .17.\"</ex>"},
 {f:"6. Interpret the confidence level", b:"Describe the procedure: in repeated samples of the same size, about 90% of intervals built this way would contain the parameter. Not \"90% probability that p is in (.303, .457)\".", s:"It's about the method working 90% of the time.<ex>\"If we repeated this many times, 90% of the intervals would contain the true rate.\"</ex>"},
 {f:"7. Judge a claim with the interval", b:"Inside: compatible but not confirmed (other values are compatible too). Outside: not supported at that confidence level.", s:"Inside = possible, not proven. Outside = not supported.<ex>\"0.45 is inside, so it's possible, but so are 0.35 and 0.40.\"</ex>"},
 {f:"8. Hypotheses about the parameter", b:"Claim in the alternative, direction matching the question; say which tail the test uses.", s:"H0 with =, HA with the claim's direction, about μ.<ex>H0: μ = 30, HA: μ > 30 (right-tailed).</ex>"},
 {f:"9. Justify z or t", b:"z when σ is known (or for a proportion); t when σ is estimated by s, with n − 1 d.f.", s:"Say why: \"σ is unknown, only s is given, so t with 14 d.f.\"."},
 {f:"10. State and check assumptions", b:"A random sample, and n ≥ 30 or an approximately normal population. With a small sample, say the test relies on the population being approximately normal.", s:"Random + (big or bell-shaped). Small sample → \"assuming the population is approximately normal\"."},
 {f:"11. A complete rejection region", b:"Tail area, d.f., critical value from the table, and the rule as an inequality: t > 1.345.", s:"\"Right tail .10, 14 d.f., t = 1.345, reject if t > 1.345.\""},
 {f:"12. A four-part conclusion", b:"Comparison (2.408 > 1.345), decision (reject / fail to reject H0), significance level, sentence in context. Never \"accept H0\".", s:"Compare, decide, α, real-world sentence."},
 {f:"13. Bracket and interpret the p-value", b:"Show the table values: P(T > 2.145) = .025 and P(T > 2.624) = .01, so .01 &lt; p-value &lt; .025. Interpret with either reading: probability assuming H0, or the α levels that would reject. Never the probability H0 is true.", s:"Two table numbers around your t, then one interpretation."},
 {f:"14. Rounding", b:"Follow the question; otherwise z-scores to two decimals for the table, standard errors with three significant digits, probabilities to four decimals.", s:"z: 2 decimals. Probabilities: 4 decimals. SE: keep 3 meaningful digits."}
]},
{id:"errors", title:"Common traps", sub:"The mistakes most students make first", cards:[
 {f:"Trap: σ instead of σ/√n", b:"Standardizing a sample mean with σ. Divide by the standard error.", s:"For averages, divide by σ/√n.<ex>Not (503 − 500)/12, but (503 − 500)/(12/6).</ex>"},
 {f:"Trap: n vs number of samples", b:"Confusing n (size of one sample) with the number of samples, or describing the sampling distribution as the distribution of the data in the sample.", s:"n = items in your one sample. The sampling distribution is about imaginary repeats."},
 {f:"Trap: n ≥ 30 as a law", b:"In either direction: assuming it guarantees normality for an extremely skewed population, or that n &lt; 30 rules out the t-test when the population is approximately normal.", s:"30 is a guideline, not magic."},
 {f:"Trap: big sample fixes bias", b:"Assuming a large non-random sample is fine. The CLT is about sampling variability, not selection bias.", s:"Size doesn't fix who you asked."},
 {f:"Trap: \"90% probability p is in the interval\"", b:"The parameter is fixed; the 90% describes the procedure.", s:"Say \"90% of intervals made this way contain p\"."},
 {f:"Trap: wrong tail area for z<sub>α/2</sub>", b:"For 90% confidence, the right tail is .05, so look up .95 (not .90, not .10).", s:"Half of what's left over goes in each tail."},
 {f:"Trap: hypotheses about x̄", b:"Writing H0: x̄ = 1, putting the claim in H0, or a direction that doesn't match the question.", s:"Use μ or p, claim in HA, matching direction."},
 {f:"Trap: z with s, or t with σ", b:"Using z when σ is unknown and s is given, or t when σ is known.", s:"Known σ → z. Only s → t."},
 {f:"Trap: n degrees of freedom", b:"Using n instead of n − 1.", s:"15 observations → 14 d.f."},
 {f:"Trap: wrong tails for the critical value", b:"Using a two-tailed critical value for a one-tailed test, or the reverse.", s:"One tail → column α. Two tails → column α/2."},
 {f:"Trap: \"accept H0\"", b:"Treating failure to reject as evidence that H0 is true; also \"the claim is inside the interval, so it's correct\".", s:"Not enough evidence ≠ proof of the opposite."},
 {f:"Trap: p-value = P(H0 true)", b:"A p-value is computed assuming H0 is true, so it can't be the probability that H0 is true.", s:"It's about the data, assuming H0, not about H0 itself."},
 {f:"Trap: backwards bracket", b:"Treating the bracket as one number, or getting it backwards: a larger t gives a smaller tail area.", s:"Further out t → smaller p."},
 {f:"Trap: table as area between 0 and z", b:"The z table gives the area to the left of z.", s:"From the far left up to z."},
 {f:"Trap: two tails", b:"Adding two tails without noticing one is ≈ 0, or not adding them when the event is \"below a or above b\".", s:"\"Or\" → add both tails; say if one is ≈ 0."},
 {f:"Trap: no context", b:"Giving a decision with no statement in the language of the problem.", s:"Always end with what it means for the business."}
]},
{id:"formulas", title:"Formulas", sub:"What's given and what you must know", cards:[
 {f:"On the formula sheet", b:"Z = (X − μ)/σ · X̄ ~ N(μ, σ/√n), z = (x̄ − μ)/(σ/√n) · p̂ ~ N(p, √(p(1 − p)/n)), z = (p̂ − p)/√(p(1 − p)/n) · α = 1 − CL · x̄ ± z<sub>α/2</sub>σ/√n · x̄ ± t<sub>α/2</sub>s/√n (n − 1 d.f.) · σ<sub>p̂</sub> = √(p(1 − p)/n) ≈ √(p̂(1 − p̂)/n) · p̂ ± z<sub>α/2</sub>√(p̂(1 − p̂)/n) · t = (x̄ − μ)/(s/√n), d.f. = n − 1 · two-sample formulas (not on this test) · z table · t table",
  s:"The sheet has the distributions, interval formulas, the t statistic and both tables. You don't need to memorize those."},
 {f:"Memorize: normal-approximation conditions", b:"Random sample, and n ≥ 30 or approximately normal population (mean); n ≥ 30, n·p̂ ≥ 15, n(1 − p̂) ≥ 15 (proportion).", s:"Random + 30+ (or bell-shaped). Proportions: 15 yes and 15 no."},
 {f:"Memorize: critical values from the tables", b:"z<sub>α/2</sub>: look up 1 − α/2 in the z table. t<sub>α/2</sub>: t table, column α/2, row n − 1. One-tailed test: column α.", s:"z: inside the table. t: column heading is the tail area."},
 {f:"Memorize: margin of error", b:"Margin of error = critical value × standard error.", s:"z (or t) times SE."},
 {f:"Memorize: control chart limits", b:"x-bar: μ ± 3σ/√n. p chart: p̄ ± 3√(p̄(1 − p̄)/n), negative LCL set to 0.", s:"Middle ± 3 standard errors."},
 {f:"Memorize: capability", b:"Cp = (USL − LSL)/(6σ). Cpk = min[(USL − μ)/(3σ), (μ − LSL)/(3σ)].", s:"Spec width over 6σ; Cpk uses the closer side over 3σ."},
 {f:"Common lookups", b:"<table class=\"dt sm\"><tr><th>CL</th><th>α/2</th><th>look up</th><th>z</th></tr><tr><td>90%</td><td>.05</td><td>.95</td><td>1.64 / 1.65</td></tr><tr><td>95%</td><td>.025</td><td>.975</td><td>1.96</td></tr><tr><td>99%</td><td>.005</td><td>.995</td><td>2.57 / 2.58</td></tr></table>3-sigma false alarm: 2(1 − .9987) = .0026.",
  s:"90 → 1.64, 95 → 1.96, 99 → 2.58. Worth memorizing."}
]},
{id:"examples", title:"Posted practice test", sub:"The three problems with model answers", cards:[
 {f:"Q1 (a): within .0001 inch", b:"Random sample of 64 bearings, σ = .001 (long run). P(μ − .0001 ≤ X̄ ≤ μ + .0001)?<ex>By the CLT, random and n ≥ 30, X̄ ~ N(μ, σ/√n). SE = .001/√64 = .000125. z = .0001/.000125 = 0.8. P(−0.8 ≤ Z ≤ 0.8) = 2(.7881 − .5) = .5762.</ex>[[normal:0,1|btw=-0.8,0.8|cap=.5762]]",
  s:"Turn ±.0001 into ±0.8 standard errors, then take the middle of the bell: 57.6%."},
 {f:"Q1 (b): somewhat skewed?", b:"<ex>We need a random sample and an approximately normal population OR n large enough (n ≥ 30). n = 64 is large enough, so a somewhat skewed population is no problem: the answer is still valid. If it were extremely skewed we'd need more caution and would check the shape of the sample data.</ex>",
  s:"64 is plenty for mild skew. Very skewed: be careful and say why."},
 {f:"Q1 (c): the decision rule", b:"Out of control if x̄ &lt; .4994 or x̄ > .5006; true mean .501.<ex>P(X̄ &lt; .4994) + P(X̄ > .5006) (mutually exclusive). P(X̄ > .5006) = P(Z > (.5006 − .501)/.000125) = P(Z > −3.2) = P(Z ≤ 3.2) ≈ .9993 (at least .9990 from the table). P(X̄ &lt; .4994) = P(Z &lt; −12.8) ≈ 0. Total ≈ .9993: extremely likely the test signals out of control.</ex>",
  s:"The machine really drifted, so the alarm goes off 99.9% of the time. That's the rule's power."},
 {f:"Q2 (a): distribution of p̂", b:"41 hooks in 108 drives, random.<ex>By the CLT, random sample and n·p̂ = 41 > 15, n(1 − p̂) = 67 > 15, so p̂ ~ N(p, √(p(1 − p)/n)). p̂ = 41/108 ≈ .38, σ<sub>p̂</sub> ≈ √(.38(.62)/108) ≈ .047. Normal (not t), since for a proportion we estimate the SD with p̂.</ex>",
  s:"Enough hooks and non-hooks → bell curve around his true hook rate."},
 {f:"Q2 (b): 90% interval", b:"<ex>p̂ ± z<sub>α/2</sub>√(p̂(1 − p̂)/n). α/2 = (1 − .9)/2 = .05, so P(Z &lt; z) = .95; closest .9495 → z = 1.64. UL = .38 + 1.64(.047) ≈ .457, LL ≈ .303. We are 90% confident the true proportion of hooks is between .303 and .457.</ex>",
  s:".38 ± 1.64 × .047 → .303 to .457."},
 {f:"Q2 (c)–(d): meaning, Adam's 45%", b:"<ex>(c) If we repeated this indefinitely, collecting samples of 108 and computing intervals, 90% of them would contain the true proportion and 10% would not. (d) .45 lies within the interval, so it doesn't contradict the data, but that doesn't show he's right: .35 and .40 are compatible too.</ex>",
  s:"90% = the method's hit rate. 45% is possible, not proven."},
 {f:"Q3 (a)–(c): hypotheses, z or t, assumptions", b:"15 tech stocks, x̄ = 1.23, s = .37.<ex>(a) μ = true mean beta of tech stocks; right-tailed: H0: μ = 1, HA: μ > 1. (b) No z: σ is unknown. (c) t needs a random sample and n ≥ 30 (not met, n = 15) or an approximately normal population, so the betas must be approximately normal.</ex>",
  s:"Claim \"riskier\" → μ > 1. Only s → t. Small n → need bell-shaped betas."},
 {f:"Q3 (d)–(f): region, decision, p-value", b:"<ex>(d) Right tail .1, 14 d.f.: t<sub>.1</sub> = 1.345; reject if t > 1.345. (e) t = (1.23 − 1)/(.37/√15) ≈ 2.408 > 1.345: reject H0. At the 10% level, enough evidence that the average tech stock is riskier than the market. (f) P(T > 2.145) = .025, P(T > 2.624) = .01 → .01 &lt; p &lt; .025. If the true mean beta were 1, a t of 2.408 or larger would occur with probability between .01 and .025; we'd reject for any α above .025.</ex>[[tdist:14|gt=1.345|obs=2.408|cap=t = 2.408 lands in the rejection region t > 1.345]]",
  s:"2.4 beats 1.345, so reject. p is between 1% and 2.5%."}
]}
];

